<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Deluge</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1655">1655</a>–<a href="/years/1660">1660</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Deluge · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-northern-war">Second Northern War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Deluge refers to the Swedish invasion and occupation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> between 1655 and 1660, alongside wider wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and others. The devastation of towns, population and economy was severe, and the episode is regarded as a turning point in the decline of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-deluge.jpg" alt="The Deluge" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Léon Comerre · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Le_d%C3%A9luge_-_mus%C3%A9e_de_beaux_arts_de_Nantes_20091017.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
